## Migration: `TimedExecutor` → .NET 8

Your code uses the **timeout enforcement** pattern — `Thread.Abort()` to kill work exceeding a deadline. In .NET 6+, `Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` (`SYSLIB0006`). The fix is cooperative cancellation with `CancellationToken`.

### Problems in the original code

| Issue | Why it breaks on .NET 8 |
|-------|------------------------|
| `thread.Abort()` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | Never thrown — dead code |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Also throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` |

### Migrated code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    /// <summary>
    /// Executes <paramref name="work"/> with a timeout.
    /// The work must cooperate by checking the <see cref="CancellationToken"/>.
    /// </summary>
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(
        Func<CancellationToken, T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);

        var task = Task.Run(() => work(cts.Token), cts.Token);

        try
        {
            return task.GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }
}
```

### Usage — before vs. after

```csharp
// ─── .NET Framework (old) ───
var executor = new TimedExecutor();
var result = executor.ExecuteWithTimeout(
    () => ExpensiveWork(),
    TimeSpan.FromSeconds(10));

// ─── .NET 8 (new) ───
var executor = new TimedExecutor();
var result = executor.ExecuteWithTimeout(
    ct => ExpensiveWork(ct),   // work must accept the token
    TimeSpan.FromSeconds(10));
```

### Critical: The work must cooperate

Cooperative cancellation only works if `work` actually checks the token. If it doesn't, the timeout **will not stop it** — the task keeps running in the background.

```csharp
// ✅ Good — checks the token at regular intervals
int ExpensiveWork(CancellationToken ct)
{
    var sum = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < 10_000_000; i++)
    {
        ct.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();  // checkpoint
        sum += Compute(i);
    }
    return sum;
}

// ❌ Bad — ignores the token; timeout has no effect
int ExpensiveWork(CancellationToken ct)
{
    return VeryLongBlockingCall(); // never checks ct
}
```

### What if the work can't be modified?

If you call uncooperative code (third-party library, native call), move it to a **child process** and use `Process.Kill()`:

```csharp
// Last resort for uncooperative code
var proc = Process.Start("worker.exe", args);
if (!proc.WaitForExit(timeout))
{
    proc.Kill(entireProcessTree: true);
    throw new TimeoutException("Worker process timed out");
}
```

### Key pitfalls

| Pitfall | Fix |
|---------|-----|
| Not passing `CancellationToken` through the full call chain | Every method in the chain must forward the token |
| Expecting `Thread.Sleep` to be cancellable | Replace with `Task.Delay(ms, ct)` or `ct.WaitHandle.WaitOne(ms)` |
| Forgetting to dispose `CancellationTokenSource` | Always wrap in `using` — leaking it leaks timers |
| Catching `OperationCanceledException` and swallowing it | Let it propagate; only catch at the orchestration point |
| Assuming cancellation is instant | It only fires at the next `ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` call |
